IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `adaq-dates`

-- Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (Qld) -- the provisions a person bringing a discrimination, sexual harassment,
-- vilification or victimisation complaint meets, from the seat of THE COMPLAINANT.
--
-- SEAT: the individual who was subjected to the alleged contravention and complains about it to the Human Rights
-- Commissioner (the Queensland Human Rights Commission), and whose complaint may go on to the tribunal (QCAT, or the
-- industrial relations commission for a work-related matter). "Is what happened to me unlawful?", "Can I complain, and
-- by when?", "Can my complaint be rejected or lapse?", "How do I get it to the tribunal?", "Who has to prove what?",
-- "What can the tribunal order?". The respondent meets the same rules from the other side; the commissioner and the
-- tribunal get no seat. Every judgment the Act leaves to the commissioner or the tribunal ("reasonable", "good cause",
-- "unjustifiable hardship", "the commissioner is of the reasonable opinion") is asked as a fact, never decided.
--
-- Every exported question takes plain facts (yes/no, dates, numbers, a choice from a list). The complainant's time
-- limits are DEONTIC rules run inside (making the complaint within 1 year, asking for reasons for a rejection, agreeing
-- to a referral the respondent asked for, answering a lapse notice, applying for review of a lapse); no question takes a
-- DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Anti-Discrimination Act 1991: the questions of a complainant`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `The parties, the acts and the time rules`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- Schedule 1: "complainant means ... (c) otherwise--the person who is the subject of the alleged contravention of the
-- Act." Only the complainant's own acts are traced; the commissioner's and the respondent's are asked as facts.
DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF
    `I`

DECLARE `Act done` IS ONE OF
    `make my complaint to the commissioner`
    `ask the commissioner for written reasons for the rejection`
    `agree in writing to the complaint being referred`
    `answer the commissioner's notice`
    `apply to the tribunal to review the lapse`

-- Runs a rule for me over one act done, at a time counted in days from the rule's start (a time before the start counts
-- as the start). On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH. It takes a DEONTIC
-- value, so it is never exported. An act on the last day of the period is in time.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
      `the act` IS AN `Act done`
      `the time it was done` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `done in time under` `the rule` `the act` `at` `the time it was done` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the time counted`), (EVENT `I` `the act` `the time counted`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHERE
        `the time counted` MEANS
            IF `the time it was done` LESS THAN 0
            THEN 0
            ELSE `the time it was done`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 7, 8 -- the attributes the Act protects`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 7: "The Act prohibits discrimination on the basis of the following attributes-- (a) sex; (b) relationship status;
-- (c) pregnancy; (d) parental status; (e) breastfeeding; (f) age; (g) race; (h) impairment; (i) religious belief or
-- religious activity; (j) political belief or activity; (k) trade union activity; (l) sex work activity; (m) gender
-- identity; (n) sexuality; (o) sex characteristics; (p) family responsibilities; (q) association with, or relation to,
-- a person identified on the basis of any of the above attributes." Schedule 1: "attribute means an attribute set out
-- in section 7" (a "means" definition: what is not in the list is not an attribute).
DECLARE `Attribute` IS ONE OF
    `sex`
    `relationship status`
    `pregnancy`
    `parental status`
    `breastfeeding`
    `age`
    `race`
    `impairment`
    `religious belief or religious activity`
    `political belief or activity`
    `trade union activity`
    `sex work activity`
    `gender identity`
    `sexuality`
    `sex characteristics`
    `family responsibilities`
    `another characteristic, not listed in section 7`

-- s 8: "Discrimination on the basis of an attribute includes direct and indirect discrimination on the basis of-- (a) a
-- characteristic that a person with any of the attributes generally has; or (b) a characteristic that is often imputed
-- to a person with any of the attributes; or (c) an attribute that a person is presumed to have, or to have had at any
-- time, by the person discriminating; or (d) an attribute that a person had, even if the person did not have it at the
-- time of the discrimination." With s 7(q) (association or relation).
DECLARE `How the attribute concerns me` IS ONE OF
    `I have the attribute`
    `I had the attribute in the past`
    `the person presumed I have, or had, the attribute`
    `it is a characteristic that people with the attribute generally have`
    `it is a characteristic often imputed to people with the attribute`
    `a person I am associated with, or related to, has the attribute`
    `some other link`

DECLARE `Is it a protected attribute` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Act prohibits discrimination on the basis of this attribute (ss 7, 8)`
    `no: section 7 lists the attributes the Act protects, and this is not one of them`
    `not covered: section 8 says what discrimination on the basis of an attribute includes, and this link is not one of its paragraphs`

@export Is the reason I was treated badly an attribute the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 protects (ss 7, 8)? The Act protects sex, relationship status, pregnancy, parental status, breastfeeding, age, race, impairment, religious belief or activity, political belief or activity, trade union activity, sex work activity, gender identity, sexuality, sex characteristics and family responsibilities, and association with a person who has one of them; it also covers a past attribute, a presumed one, and characteristics generally had by or imputed to people with it.
GIVEN `the attribute` IS AN `Attribute` @desc The attribute you say you were treated badly because of. One of the s 7 attributes, or another characteristic not listed in section 7
      `how it concerns me` IS A `How the attribute concerns me` @desc How the attribute relates to you: you have it; you had it in the past; the person presumed you have or had it; it is a characteristic people with the attribute generally have; it is a characteristic often imputed to them; a person you are associated with or related to has it; or some other link
GIVETH AN `Is it a protected attribute`
`Is the ground of my complaint a protected attribute` `the attribute` `how it concerns me` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the attribute`
    WHEN `another characteristic, not listed in section 7` THEN `no: section 7 lists the attributes the Act protects, and this is not one of them`
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER `how it concerns me`
        WHEN `some other link` THEN `not covered: section 8 says what discrimination on the basis of an attribute includes, and this link is not one of its paragraphs`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the Act prohibits discrimination on the basis of this attribute (ss 7, 8)`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 9, 10, 11 -- direct and indirect discrimination`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 10(1): "Direct discrimination on the basis of an attribute happens if a person treats, or proposes to treat, a person
-- with an attribute less favourably than another person without the attribute is or would be treated in circumstances
-- that are the same or not materially different." (2): the discriminator need not consider the treatment less
-- favourable. (3): "The person's motive for discriminating is irrelevant." (4): "If there are 2 or more reasons ... the
-- person treats the other person less favourably on the basis of the attribute if the attribute is a substantial reason
-- for the treatment." (5): needing special services or facilities is irrelevant to the comparison.
DECLARE `The part the attribute played` IS ONE OF
    `it was the only reason`
    `it was one of 2 or more reasons, and a substantial reason`
    `it was one of 2 or more reasons, but not a substantial reason`
    `it was not a reason`

@export Was I directly discriminated against, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 10? Direct discrimination happens when a person treats you, or proposes to treat you, less favourably than they treat or would treat a person without your attribute in the same or not materially different circumstances, because of the attribute. Their motive does not matter, nor whether they thought the treatment less favourable; if there were several reasons, the attribute must be a substantial one.
GIVEN `treated less favourably` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were treated, or someone proposed to treat you, less favourably than a person without your attribute is or would be treated in circumstances that are the same or not materially different (any need you have for special services or facilities is left out of the comparison, s 10(5))
      `the part it played` IS A `The part the attribute played` @desc The part your attribute played in the treatment: it was the only reason; one of 2 or more reasons and a substantial reason; one of 2 or more reasons but not a substantial reason; or not a reason
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was I directly discriminated against` `treated less favourably` `the part it played` IF
        `treated less favourably`
    AND (CONSIDER `the part it played`
         WHEN `it was the only reason` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `it was one of 2 or more reasons, and a substantial reason` THEN TRUE
         OTHERWISE FALSE)

-- s 11(1): "Indirect discrimination on the basis of an attribute happens if a person imposes, or proposes to impose, a
-- term-- (a) with which a person with an attribute does not or is not able to comply; and (b) with which a higher
-- proportion of people without the attribute comply or are able to comply; and (c) that is not reasonable." (2): whether
-- a term is reasonable depends on all the circumstances, including the consequences of failure to comply, the cost of
-- alternative terms and the imposer's financial circumstances. (3): the imposer need not be aware. (4): "term includes
-- condition, requirement or practice, whether or not written."
@export Was I indirectly discriminated against, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 11? Indirect discrimination happens when a person imposes, or proposes to impose, a term (a condition, requirement or practice, written or not) that you, having an attribute, do not or cannot comply with, that a higher proportion of people without the attribute comply or can comply with, and that is not reasonable. The person need not be aware of the effect.
GIVEN `a term was imposed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person imposed, or proposes to impose, a term on you: a condition, requirement or practice, written or not
      `I cannot comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, having the attribute, do not or are not able to comply with the term
      `a higher proportion can comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a higher proportion of people without the attribute comply, or are able to comply, with the term
      `the term is not reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term is not reasonable, if the tribunal so finds, weighing all the circumstances, including the consequences of not complying, the cost of other terms and the financial circumstances of the person imposing it (s 11(2))
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was I indirectly discriminated against` `a term was imposed` `I cannot comply` `a higher proportion can comply` `the term is not reasonable` IF
        `a term was imposed`
    AND `I cannot comply`
    AND `a higher proportion can comply`
    AND `the term is not reasonable`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Chapter 2, part 4 -- the areas of activity`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 6(2)(a): discrimination is prohibited when it is on a ground in part 2, of a type in part 3 and "in an area of
-- activity set out in part 4". The areas, with the section that sets each prohibition.
DECLARE `Area` IS ONE OF
    `seeking work, or being offered work (s 14)`
    `at work, as a worker (s 15)`
    `working for a principal under a contract or arrangement with a third person (s 15A)`
    `being invited to become a partner, or as a partner (ss 16, 17, 18)`
    `joining, or as a member of, an organisation of workers, employers, or people in an industry, profession, trade or business (ss 19, 20)`
    `getting, keeping or holding a qualification or authorisation needed for a profession, trade or business (ss 21, 22)`
    `using an employment agency (s 23)`
    `applying to, or studying at, an educational institution (ss 38, 39)`
    `being supplied goods or services (s 46)`
    `superannuation (ss 53 to 57)`
    `insurance (ss 67 to 71)`
    `being offered, or refused, an interest in land (s 77)`
    `applying for, renting or living in accommodation (ss 82 to 85)`
    `applying to join, or as a member of, a club (ss 94, 95)`
    `dealing with a person administering a State law or a State Government program (s 101)`
    `as a member of a local government, in the performance of official functions (s 102)`
    `somewhere else`

-- The work and work-related area: ss 14 to 23 (Division 2, subdivision 1).
GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the area` `is the work or work-related area` IF
    CONSIDER `the area`
    WHEN `seeking work, or being offered work (s 14)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `at work, as a worker (s 15)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `working for a principal under a contract or arrangement with a third person (s 15A)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `being invited to become a partner, or as a partner (ss 16, 17, 18)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `joining, or as a member of, an organisation of workers, employers, or people in an industry, profession, trade or business (ss 19, 20)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `getting, keeping or holding a qualification or authorisation needed for a profession, trade or business (ss 21, 22)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `using an employment agency (s 23)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the area` `is the pre-work or work area` IF
    CONSIDER `the area`
    WHEN `seeking work, or being offered work (s 14)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `at work, as a worker (s 15)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the area` `is the education area` IF
    CONSIDER `the area`
    WHEN `applying to, or studying at, an educational institution (ss 38, 39)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the area` `is the goods and services area` IF
    CONSIDER `the area`
    WHEN `being supplied goods or services (s 46)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the area` `is the accommodation area` IF
    CONSIDER `the area`
    WHEN `applying for, renting or living in accommodation (ss 82 to 85)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the area` `is the club area` IF
    CONSIDER `the area`
    WHEN `applying to join, or as a member of, a club (ss 94, 95)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Is the area covered` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Act prohibits discrimination in this area of activity (chapter 2, part 4)`
    `no: the partnership sections apply only to a partnership of 6 or more people (ss 16, 17, 18, 56, 57, 70, 71)`
    `no: a non-profit association established for a social, literary, cultural, political, sporting, recreational, community service or similar purpose is not a supplier of goods or services (s 46(2))`
    `no: an association is a club only if it carries out its purposes for the purpose of making a profit (schedule 1, definition of club)`
    `no: discrimination on the basis of trade union activity is excluded where the Industrial Relations Act 2016, chapter 12, part 9, division 2 or part 10 applies (ss 19(2), 20(2))`
    `no: discrimination between local government members on the basis of political belief or activity is excluded (s 102(2))`
    `no: the Act prohibits discrimination only in the areas of activity set out in chapter 2, part 4 (s 6(2)(a)(iii))`

@export Did the discrimination happen in an area of activity where the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 prohibits it (chapter 2, part 4)? The areas are work and work-related areas (seeking work, work, principals, partnerships of 6 or more, industrial and professional organisations, qualifying bodies, employment agencies), education, goods and services, superannuation, insurance, land, accommodation, clubs, the administration of State laws and programs, and local government.
GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area` @desc Where the discrimination happened. One of the part 4 areas listed, or somewhere else
      `the attribute` IS AN `Attribute` @desc The attribute the discrimination was on the basis of
      `people in the partnership` IS A NUMBER @desc For a partnership: how many people are, or will be, in the partnership (0 if not a partnership)
      `the association is non-profit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For goods and services or a club: whether the supplier or club is an association established for social, literary, cultural, political, sporting, athletic, recreational, community service or similar lawful purposes that does not carry out its purposes to make a profit
      `the Industrial Relations Act provisions apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For trade union activity in joining or belonging to an organisation: whether the Industrial Relations Act 2016, chapter 12, part 9, division 2 or part 10 applies to the conduct
GIVETH AN `Is the area covered`
`Is the area covered by the Act` `the area` `the attribute` `people in the partnership` `the association is non-profit` `the Industrial Relations Act provisions apply` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the area`
    WHEN `somewhere else` THEN `no: the Act prohibits discrimination only in the areas of activity set out in chapter 2, part 4 (s 6(2)(a)(iii))`
    WHEN `being invited to become a partner, or as a partner (ss 16, 17, 18)` THEN
        IF `people in the partnership` AT LEAST 6
        THEN `yes: the Act prohibits discrimination in this area of activity (chapter 2, part 4)`
        ELSE `no: the partnership sections apply only to a partnership of 6 or more people (ss 16, 17, 18, 56, 57, 70, 71)`
    WHEN `joining, or as a member of, an organisation of workers, employers, or people in an industry, profession, trade or business (ss 19, 20)` THEN
        IF `the Industrial Relations Act provisions apply` AND `it is trade union activity`
        THEN `no: discrimination on the basis of trade union activity is excluded where the Industrial Relations Act 2016, chapter 12, part 9, division 2 or part 10 applies (ss 19(2), 20(2))`
        ELSE `yes: the Act prohibits discrimination in this area of activity (chapter 2, part 4)`
    WHEN `being supplied goods or services (s 46)` THEN
        IF `the association is non-profit`
        THEN `no: a non-profit association established for a social, literary, cultural, political, sporting, recreational, community service or similar purpose is not a supplier of goods or services (s 46(2))`
        ELSE `yes: the Act prohibits discrimination in this area of activity (chapter 2, part 4)`
    WHEN `applying to join, or as a member of, a club (ss 94, 95)` THEN
        IF `the association is non-profit`
        THEN `no: an association is a club only if it carries out its purposes for the purpose of making a profit (schedule 1, definition of club)`
        ELSE `yes: the Act prohibits discrimination in this area of activity (chapter 2, part 4)`
    WHEN `as a member of a local government, in the performance of official functions (s 102)` THEN
        IF `it is political belief or activity`
        THEN `no: discrimination between local government members on the basis of political belief or activity is excluded (s 102(2))`
        ELSE `yes: the Act prohibits discrimination in this area of activity (chapter 2, part 4)`
    OTHERWISE `yes: the Act prohibits discrimination in this area of activity (chapter 2, part 4)`
    WHERE
        `it is trade union activity` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the attribute`
            WHEN `trade union activity` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `it is political belief or activity` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the attribute`
            WHEN `political belief or activity` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Chapter 2, parts 4 and 5 -- the exemptions most often relied on (s 205: the respondent must prove them)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `Exemption` IS ONE OF
    `a genuine occupational requirement for the position (s 25(1))`
    `the employer's religious beliefs (s 25(3))`
    `youth wages (s 33)`
    `special services or facilities, or the circumstances of an impairment, would impose unjustifiable hardship (ss 35, 36, 44, 51, 92, 100)`
    `age-based benefits and concessions (s 49)`
    `shared accommodation in the main home (s 87)`
    `welfare measures (s 104)`
    `equal opportunity measures (s 105)`
    `acts done to comply with legislation or a court order (s 106)`
    `public health (s 107)`
    `workplace health and safety (s 108)`
    `a body established for religious purposes acting in accordance with its doctrine (s 109(1)(d))`
    `restrictions on competitive sport (s 111)`
    `a legal incapacity relevant to the transaction (s 112)`

DECLARE `Does the exemption apply` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the exemption applies, so the discrimination is not unlawful (the respondent must prove it, s 205)`
    `no: this exemption does not reach discrimination in this area of activity`
    `no: this exemption does not reach discrimination on this attribute`
    `no: the exemption's conditions are not met or not proved (s 205)`

-- Each exemption in its own words:
-- s 25(1) "A person may impose genuine occupational requirements for a position." (the work and work-related area,
--   s 24);
-- s 25(3) a religious employer may discriminate "with respect to a matter that is otherwise prohibited under section 14
--   or 15, in a way that is not unreasonable", where the person openly acts contrary to the employer's religious beliefs
--   and acting consistently with them is a genuine occupational requirement; (6) "Subsection (3) does not apply to
--   discrimination on the basis of age, race or impairment";
-- s 33 "A person may remunerate a worker who is under 21 years of age according to the worker's age.";
-- ss 35, 36 (work), 44 (education), 51 (goods and services), 92 (accommodation), 100 (club, failing to accept an
--   application for membership): discrimination on the basis of impairment, unjustifiable hardship (s 5);
-- s 49 benefits and concessions on the basis of age (goods and services);
-- s 87 deciding who is to reside in accommodation that forms part of the main home of the person or a near relative and
--   "is for no more than 3 people other than" that person or near relatives;
-- ss 104 to 108, 112: any area (s 103);
-- s 109(1)(d) an act by a body established for religious purposes, in accordance with its doctrine and necessary to
--   avoid offending religious sensitivities; (2) "does not apply in the work or work-related area or in the education
--   area";
-- s 111(1) restricting participation in a competitive sporting activity (a) to males or females, reasonably, having
--   regard to strength, stamina or physique, but (2) "Subsection (1)(a) does not apply to a sporting activity for
--   children who are less than 12 years of age"; (b) to people who can effectively compete; (c) to people of a
--   specified age; (d) to people with an impairment; (3) on the basis of gender identity, reasonably.
-- Each test the exemption turns on (genuine, reasonable, necessary, unjustifiable hardship, not inconsistent with the
-- Act, the main home) is one input: the respondent's proof of it (s 205(1)).
@export Does an exemption the other side relies on make the discrimination lawful, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (chapter 2, parts 4 and 5)? Covers the exemptions most often raised: genuine occupational requirements, religious employers, youth wages, unjustifiable hardship for impairment, age-based concessions, shared accommodation, welfare and equal opportunity measures, compliance with legislation, public health, workplace safety, religious bodies, competitive sport and legal incapacity. The respondent must prove an exemption (s 205).
GIVEN `the exemption` IS AN `Exemption` @desc The exemption the respondent relies on
      `the area` IS AN `Area` @desc Where the discrimination happened
      `the attribute` IS AN `Attribute` @desc The attribute the discrimination was on the basis of
      `the respondent proves its test` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent proves, on the balance of probabilities, the test the exemption sets: for example that the requirement is genuine, the hardship unjustifiable, the act reasonably necessary, the purpose not inconsistent with the Act, the restriction reasonable, or the accommodation part of the main home of the person or a near relative
      `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years (used for youth wages, s 33)
      `other people living there` IS A NUMBER @desc For shared accommodation: how many people, other than the person or near relatives whose main home it is, the accommodation is for (s 87(b))
      `a sporting activity for children under 12` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For sport: whether the sporting activity is for children who are less than 12 years old (s 111(2))
GIVETH A `Does the exemption apply`
`Does the exemption apply to me` `the exemption` `the area` `the attribute` `the respondent proves its test` `my age` `other people living there` `a sporting activity for children under 12` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the exemption`
    WHEN `a genuine occupational requirement for the position (s 25(1))` THEN
        IF `the area` `is the work or work-related area`
        THEN `the test`
        ELSE `not this area`
    WHEN `the employer's religious beliefs (s 25(3))` THEN
        IF NOT (`the area` `is the pre-work or work area`)
        THEN `not this area`
        ELSE
            IF `it is age, race or impairment`
            THEN `not this attribute`
            ELSE `the test`
    WHEN `youth wages (s 33)` THEN
        IF NOT (`the area` `is the work or work-related area`)
        THEN `not this area`
        ELSE
            IF NOT (`it is age`)
            THEN `not this attribute`
            ELSE
                IF `my age` LESS THAN 21
                THEN `yes: the exemption applies, so the discrimination is not unlawful (the respondent must prove it, s 205)`
                ELSE `no: the exemption's conditions are not met or not proved (s 205)`
    WHEN `special services or facilities, or the circumstances of an impairment, would impose unjustifiable hardship (ss 35, 36, 44, 51, 92, 100)` THEN
        IF NOT (`an area with an unjustifiable hardship exemption`)
        THEN `not this area`
        ELSE
            IF NOT (`it is impairment`)
            THEN `not this attribute`
            ELSE `the test`
    WHEN `age-based benefits and concessions (s 49)` THEN
        IF NOT (`the area` `is the goods and services area`)
        THEN `not this area`
        ELSE
            IF NOT (`it is age`)
            THEN `not this attribute`
            ELSE `the test`
    WHEN `shared accommodation in the main home (s 87)` THEN
        IF NOT (`the area` `is the accommodation area`)
        THEN `not this area`
        ELSE
            IF `other people living there` AT MOST 3
            THEN `the test`
            ELSE `no: the exemption's conditions are not met or not proved (s 205)`
    WHEN `a body established for religious purposes acting in accordance with its doctrine (s 109(1)(d))` THEN
        IF (`the area` `is the work or work-related area`) OR (`the area` `is the education area`)
        THEN `not this area`
        ELSE `the test`
    WHEN `restrictions on competitive sport (s 111)` THEN
        IF `it is sex` AND `a sporting activity for children under 12`
        THEN `no: the exemption's conditions are not met or not proved (s 205)`
        ELSE `the test`
    OTHERWISE `the test`
    WHERE
        `the test` MEANS
            IF `the respondent proves its test`
            THEN `yes: the exemption applies, so the discrimination is not unlawful (the respondent must prove it, s 205)`
            ELSE `no: the exemption's conditions are not met or not proved (s 205)`
        `not this area` MEANS `no: this exemption does not reach discrimination in this area of activity`
        `not this attribute` MEANS `no: this exemption does not reach discrimination on this attribute`
        `an area with an unjustifiable hardship exemption` MEANS
               (`the area` `is the work or work-related area`)
            OR (`the area` `is the education area`)
            OR (`the area` `is the goods and services area`)
            OR (`the area` `is the accommodation area`)
            OR (`the area` `is the club area`)
        `it is sex` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the attribute`
            WHEN `sex` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `it is age` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the attribute`
            WHEN `age` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `it is impairment` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the attribute`
            WHEN `impairment` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE
        `it is age, race or impairment` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the attribute`
            WHEN `age` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `race` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `impairment` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Chapter 3, ss 118, 119, 120 -- sexual harassment`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 118: "A person must not sexually harass another person." (in any area, and outside them). s 119: "Sexual harassment
-- happens if a person-- (a) subjects another person to an unsolicited act of physical intimacy; or (b) makes an
-- unsolicited demand or request (whether directly or by implication) for sexual favours from the other person; or (c)
-- makes a remark with sexual connotations relating to the other person; or (d) engages in any other unwelcome conduct of
-- a sexual nature in relation to the other person; and the person ... does so-- (e) with the intention of offending,
-- humiliating or intimidating the other person; or (f) in circumstances where a reasonable person would have
-- anticipated the possibility that the other person would be offended, humiliated or intimidated by the conduct."
-- s 120: the relevant circumstances include the other person's sex, age, race, impairment, the relationship between
-- them, and any other circumstance.
DECLARE `The conduct` IS ONE OF
    `an unsolicited act of physical intimacy`
    `an unsolicited demand or request, direct or implied, for sexual favours`
    `a remark with sexual connotations relating to me`
    `other unwelcome conduct of a sexual nature in relation to me`
    `conduct of none of these kinds`

@export Was I sexually harassed, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 118 to 120? Sexual harassment is an unsolicited act of physical intimacy, an unsolicited request for sexual favours, a remark with sexual connotations about you, or other unwelcome conduct of a sexual nature, done to offend, humiliate or intimidate you, or where a reasonable person would have anticipated that you might be offended, humiliated or intimidated. It is prohibited everywhere, not only in the areas of activity.
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A `The conduct` @desc What the person did: an unsolicited act of physical intimacy; an unsolicited demand or request, direct or implied, for sexual favours; a remark with sexual connotations relating to you; other unwelcome conduct of a sexual nature in relation to you; or conduct of none of these kinds
      `meant to offend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person did it with the intention of offending, humiliating or intimidating you
      `a reasonable person would have anticipated it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, if the tribunal so finds, a reasonable person would have anticipated the possibility that you would be offended, humiliated or intimidated, given your sex, age, race, any impairment, your relationship with the person and your other circumstances (s 120)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was I sexually harassed` `the conduct` `meant to offend` `a reasonable person would have anticipated it` IF
        (CONSIDER `the conduct`
         WHEN `conduct of none of these kinds` THEN FALSE
         OTHERWISE TRUE)
    AND (`meant to offend` OR `a reasonable person would have anticipated it`)

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Chapter 4 -- requests to contravene, unnecessary information, vilification`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 122: "A person must not request or encourage another person to contravene the Act." s 123: "If-- (a) a person
-- requests or encourages another person to contravene the Act; and (b) the other person acts, or attempts to act, on
-- the request or encouragement; both are jointly and severally civilly liable for the contravention, and a proceeding
-- under the Act may be taken against either or both."
DECLARE `Is the person who encouraged it liable` IS ONE OF
    `yes: requesting or encouraging a contravention is itself a contravention (s 122), and as the other person acted or tried to act on it, both are jointly and severally liable for it, and you may complain against either or both (s 123)`
    `yes, for the request or encouragement itself (s 122); the joint liability of s 123 does not arise, as the other person did not act or try to act on it`
    `no: sections 122 and 123 reach only a person who requested or encouraged a contravention of the Act`

@export Is the person who asked or encouraged someone else to discriminate against or harass me liable too, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 122, 123? Requesting or encouraging a contravention is itself unlawful; if the other person acts or tries to act on it, both are jointly and severally liable.
GIVEN `they requested or encouraged it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person requested or encouraged another person to contravene the Act against you
      `the other person acted on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other person acted, or attempted to act, on the request or encouragement
GIVETH AN `Is the person who encouraged it liable`
`Is the person who encouraged it liable too` `they requested or encouraged it` `the other person acted on it` MEANS
    IF NOT (`they requested or encouraged it`)
    THEN `no: sections 122 and 123 reach only a person who requested or encouraged a contravention of the Act`
    ELSE
        IF `the other person acted on it`
        THEN `yes: requesting or encouraging a contravention is itself a contravention (s 122), and as the other person acted or tried to act on it, both are jointly and severally liable for it, and you may complain against either or both (s 123)`
        ELSE `yes, for the request or encouragement itself (s 122); the joint liability of s 123 does not arise, as the other person did not act or try to act on it`

-- s 124(1): "A person must not ask another person, either orally or in writing, to supply information on which
-- unlawful discrimination might be based." (2): it does not apply to a request "necessary to comply with, or ...
-- specifically authorised by" an existing provision of another Act, a court order, an existing award, an existing
-- industrial agreement, or an order of QCAT or the industrial relations commission. (3): "It is a defence ... if the
-- respondent proves, on the balance of probabilities, that the information was reasonably required for a purpose that
-- did not involve discrimination."
DECLARE `Can I be asked for the information` IS ONE OF
    `no: a person must not ask you to supply information on which unlawful discrimination might be based (s 124(1))`
    `yes: the request is necessary to comply with, or specifically authorised by, an existing provision of another Act, a court order, an existing award or industrial agreement, or an order of QCAT or the industrial relations commission (s 124(2))`
    `the asker has a defence: it proves the information was reasonably required for a purpose that did not involve discrimination (s 124(3))`
    `not covered: section 124 limits only requests for information on which unlawful discrimination might be based`

@export Can I lawfully be asked for this information, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 124? A person must not ask you for information on which unlawful discrimination might be based, unless the request is required or authorised by law or a court or tribunal order, or the asker proves it was reasonably required for a purpose that did not involve discrimination.
GIVEN `discrimination might be based on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information asked for is information on which unlawful discrimination might be based (for example about an impairment, age, religion or relationship status)
      `the law requires or authorises the request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the request is necessary to comply with, or specifically authorised by, a provision of another Act in existence when s 124 commenced, a court order, an existing minimum-wage order or award, an existing industrial agreement under the repealed Industrial Relations Act 1999, or an order of QCAT or the industrial relations commission
      `the asker proves a non-discriminatory purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who asked proves, on the balance of probabilities, that the information was reasonably required for a purpose that did not involve discrimination
GIVETH A `Can I be asked for the information`
`Can I be asked for the information` `discrimination might be based on it` `the law requires or authorises the request` `the asker proves a non-discriminatory purpose` MEANS
    IF NOT (`discrimination might be based on it`)
    THEN `not covered: section 124 limits only requests for information on which unlawful discrimination might be based`
    ELSE
        IF `the law requires or authorises the request`
        THEN `yes: the request is necessary to comply with, or specifically authorised by, an existing provision of another Act, a court order, an existing award or industrial agreement, or an order of QCAT or the industrial relations commission (s 124(2))`
        ELSE
            IF `the asker proves a non-discriminatory purpose`
            THEN `the asker has a defence: it proves the information was reasonably required for a purpose that did not involve discrimination (s 124(3))`
            ELSE `no: a person must not ask you to supply information on which unlawful discrimination might be based (s 124(1))`

-- s 124A(1): "A person must not, by a public act, incite hatred towards, serious contempt for, or severe ridicule of, a
-- person or group of persons on the ground of the race, religion, sexuality, sex characteristics or gender identity of
-- the person or members of the group." (2): it "does not make unlawful-- (a) the publication of a fair report of a public
-- act ...; or (b) the publication of material in circumstances in which the publication would be subject to a defence of
-- absolute privilege in proceedings for defamation; or (c) a public act, done reasonably and in good faith, for
-- academic, artistic, scientific or research purposes or for other purposes in the public interest, including public
-- discussion or debate about, and expositions of, any act or matter." (3): a public act includes any communication to
-- the public and conduct observable by the public, but "does not include the distribution or dissemination of any matter
-- by a person to the public if the person does not know, and could not reasonably be expected to know, the content".
DECLARE `What the public act was` IS ONE OF
    `none of the excepted kinds`
    `the publication of a fair report of a public act`
    `the publication of material that would have absolute privilege in defamation proceedings`
    `a public act done reasonably and in good faith for academic, artistic, scientific or research purposes, or other purposes in the public interest`

DECLARE `Is it unlawful vilification` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is unlawful vilification, and you may complain about it (s 124A(1))`
    `no: distributing matter whose content the person did not know, and could not reasonably be expected to know, is not a public act (s 124A(3)(b))`
    `no: section 124A(2) does not make it unlawful: a fair report, absolutely privileged material, or an act done reasonably and in good faith for academic, artistic, scientific, research or public interest purposes`
    `not covered: section 124A reaches only a public act`
    `not covered: section 124A reaches only vilification on the ground of race, religion, sexuality, sex characteristics or gender identity`
    `not covered: section 124A reaches only an act that incites hatred towards, serious contempt for, or severe ridicule of a person or group`

@export Was I, or my group, unlawfully vilified, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 124A? It is unlawful to incite, by a public act, hatred towards, serious contempt for, or severe ridicule of a person or group on the ground of race, religion, sexuality, sex characteristics or gender identity, except for fair reports, absolutely privileged material, and acts done reasonably and in good faith for academic, artistic, scientific, research or public interest purposes.
GIVEN `it was a public act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a public act: any communication to the public (speaking, writing, broadcasting, online and so on) or conduct observable by the public, including gestures and the display of clothing, signs, flags or emblems
      `an unknowing distributor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person only distributed or disseminated the matter to the public without knowing, and without being reasonably expected to know, its content
      `the ground` IS AN `Attribute` @desc The ground of the act: race, religious belief or religious activity (religion), sexuality, sex characteristics, gender identity, or another attribute
      `it incited hatred, serious contempt or severe ridicule` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act incited hatred towards, serious contempt for, or severe ridicule of you or your group
      `what it was` IS A `What the public act was` @desc Whether the act was one of the kinds s 124A(2) leaves lawful: a fair report of a public act; material with absolute privilege in defamation; an act done reasonably and in good faith for academic, artistic, scientific, research or public interest purposes; or none of these
GIVETH AN `Is it unlawful vilification`
`Was I vilified` `it was a public act` `an unknowing distributor` `the ground` `it incited hatred, serious contempt or severe ridicule` `what it was` MEANS
    IF NOT (`it was a public act`)
    THEN `not covered: section 124A reaches only a public act`
    ELSE
        IF `an unknowing distributor`
        THEN `no: distributing matter whose content the person did not know, and could not reasonably be expected to know, is not a public act (s 124A(3)(b))`
        ELSE
            IF NOT (`a vilification ground`)
            THEN `not covered: section 124A reaches only vilification on the ground of race, religion, sexuality, sex characteristics or gender identity`
            ELSE
                IF NOT (`it incited hatred, serious contempt or severe ridicule`)
                THEN `not covered: section 124A reaches only an act that incites hatred towards, serious contempt for, or severe ridicule of a person or group`
                ELSE
                    CONSIDER `what it was`
                    WHEN `none of the excepted kinds` THEN `yes: it is unlawful vilification, and you may complain about it (s 124A(1))`
                    OTHERWISE `no: section 124A(2) does not make it unlawful: a fair report, absolutely privileged material, or an act done reasonably and in good faith for academic, artistic, scientific, research or public interest purposes`
    WHERE
        `a vilification ground` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the ground`
            WHEN `race` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `religious belief or religious activity` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `sexuality` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `sex characteristics` THEN TRUE
            WHEN `gender identity` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Chapter 5 -- discriminatory advertisements and victimisation`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 127(1): "A person must not publish or display an advertisement, or authorise its publication or display, if the
-- advertisement indicates that a person intends to act in a way that contravenes the Act." (1A): "subsection (1) does
-- not apply to an advertisement so far as it advertises for a worker who is under 21 years of age". (2): "It is a
-- defence to a complaint ... if the respondent proves, on the balance of probabilities, that the respondent took
-- reasonable precautions to prevent the publication or display happening."
DECLARE `Is the advertisement unlawful` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the advertisement contravenes s 127(1), and you may complain about it`
    `no: an advertisement for a worker under 21 years of age, so far as it advertises for one, is outside s 127(1) (s 127(1A))`
    `the respondent has a defence to your complaint: it proves it took reasonable precautions to prevent the publication or display (s 127(2))`
    `not covered: section 127 reaches only an advertisement indicating an intention to act in a way that contravenes the Act`

@export Is a discriminatory advertisement unlawful, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 127? It is unlawful to publish or display an advertisement indicating an intention to act in a way that contravenes the Act, except so far as it advertises for a worker under 21; on a complaint, the respondent has a defence if it took reasonable precautions to prevent the publication.
GIVEN `it indicates an intention to contravene` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the advertisement indicates that a person intends to act in a way that contravenes the Act (for example by discriminating on an attribute in an area of activity)
      `it advertises for a worker under 21` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the advertisement advertises for a worker who is under 21 years of age (by a particular age, an age group or otherwise)
      `the respondent proves reasonable precautions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who published or displayed it proves, on the balance of probabilities, that it took reasonable precautions to prevent the publication or display
GIVETH AN `Is the advertisement unlawful`
`Is the advertisement unlawful` `it indicates an intention to contravene` `it advertises for a worker under 21` `the respondent proves reasonable precautions` MEANS
    IF NOT (`it indicates an intention to contravene`)
    THEN `not covered: section 127 reaches only an advertisement indicating an intention to act in a way that contravenes the Act`
    ELSE
        IF `it advertises for a worker under 21`
        THEN `no: an advertisement for a worker under 21 years of age, so far as it advertises for one, is outside s 127(1) (s 127(1A))`
        ELSE
            IF `the respondent proves reasonable precautions`
            THEN `the respondent has a defence to your complaint: it proves it took reasonable precautions to prevent the publication or display (s 127(2))`
            ELSE `yes: the advertisement contravenes s 127(1), and you may complain about it`

-- s 130(1): "Victimisation happens if a person (the respondent) does an act, or threatens to do an act, to the detriment
-- of another person (the complainant)-- (a) because the complainant, or a person associated with, or related to, the
-- complainant-- (i) refused to do an act that would amount to a contravention of the Act; or (ii) in good faith,
-- alleged, or intends to allege that a person committed an act that would amount to a contravention of the Act; or
-- (iii) is, has been, or intends to be, involved in a proceeding under the Act against any person; or (b) because the
-- respondent believes that the complainant, or a person associated with, or related to, the complainant is doing, has
-- done, or intends to do one of the things mentioned in paragraph (a)(i), (ii) or (iii)." (2): involvement in a
-- proceeding includes complaining, a prosecution, supplying information and being a witness. s 131: victimisation is
-- not affected by the failure or withdrawal of the complaint or proceeding.
DECLARE `Why it was done` IS ONE OF
    `because I, or someone associated with or related to me, refused to do an act that would contravene the Act`
    `because I, or someone associated with or related to me, in good faith alleged or intends to allege a contravention of the Act`
    `because I, or someone associated with or related to me, alleged a contravention of the Act, but not in good faith`
    `because I, or someone associated with or related to me, is, has been or intends to be involved in a proceeding under the Act`
    `because the person believes I, or someone associated with or related to me, did, is doing or intends to do one of those things`
    `for another reason`

@export Was I victimised, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 129 to 131? Victimisation is doing or threatening something to your detriment because you (or someone associated with or related to you) refused to contravene the Act, alleged a contravention in good faith, or took part in a proceeding under the Act (including complaining, giving information or being a witness), or because the person believes you did. It does not matter that the complaint failed or was withdrawn.
GIVEN `a detriment was done or threatened` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person did an act, or threatened to do an act, to your detriment
      `why it was done` IS A `Why it was done` @desc Why the person did or threatened it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was I victimised` `a detriment was done or threatened` `why it was done` IF
        `a detriment was done or threatened`
    AND (CONSIDER `why it was done`
         WHEN `because I, or someone associated with or related to me, alleged a contravention of the Act, but not in good faith` THEN FALSE
         WHEN `for another reason` THEN FALSE
         OTHERWISE TRUE)

-- s 129: "A person must not victimise another person. Maximum penalty-- (a) in the case of an individual--45 penalty
-- units or imprisonment for 3 months; or (b) in the case of a corporation--170 penalty units." The value of a penalty
-- unit is set by the Penalties and Sentences Act 1992 and its regulation, not deposited.
DECLARE `Maximum penalty` HAS
    `the penalty units` IS A NUMBER
    `the months of imprisonment` IS A NUMBER

@export What is the most the person who victimised me can be penalised, as an offence, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 129? An individual: 45 penalty units or 3 months' imprisonment; a corporation: 170 penalty units. (The dollar value of a penalty unit is set elsewhere.) Victimisation can also be the subject of your complaint.
GIVEN `the person is a corporation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who victimised you is a corporation (rather than an individual)
GIVETH A `Maximum penalty`
`What is the most the person who victimised me can be penalised` `the person is a corporation` MEANS
    IF `the person is a corporation`
    THEN `Maximum penalty` WITH `the penalty units` IS 170, `the months of imprisonment` IS 0
    ELSE `Maximum penalty` WITH `the penalty units` IS 45, `the months of imprisonment` IS 3

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Chapter 6, s 133 -- liability for workers and agents`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 133(1): "If any of a person's workers or agents contravenes the Act in the course of work or while acting as agent,
-- both the person and the worker or agent ... are jointly and severally civilly liable for the contravention, and a
-- proceeding under the Act may be taken against either or both." (2): "It is a defence to a proceeding for a
-- contravention of the Act arising under subsection (1) if the respondent proves, on the balance of probabilities, that
-- the respondent took reasonable steps to prevent the worker or agent contravening the Act."
DECLARE `Is the employer liable too` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the employer or principal and its worker or agent are jointly and severally liable, and you may complain against either or both (s 133(1))`
    `no: the employer or principal has a defence, having proved it took reasonable steps to prevent the contravention (s 133(2)); the worker or agent remains liable`
    `not covered: section 133 applies only to a contravention by a worker or agent in the course of work or while acting as agent`

@export Is the employer (or principal) of the person who discriminated against or harassed me liable too, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 133? Yes, jointly with its worker or agent, if the contravention was in the course of work or while acting as agent, unless the employer proves it took reasonable steps to prevent it.
GIVEN `a worker or agent did it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who contravened the Act was a worker or agent of the employer or principal
      `in the course of work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it happened in the course of the worker's work, or while the agent was acting as agent
      `the employer proves reasonable steps` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer or principal proves, on the balance of probabilities, that it took reasonable steps to prevent the worker or agent contravening the Act
GIVETH AN `Is the employer liable too`
`Is the employer liable too` `a worker or agent did it` `in the course of work` `the employer proves reasonable steps` MEANS
    IF NOT (`a worker or agent did it` AND `in the course of work`)
    THEN `not covered: section 133 applies only to a contravention by a worker or agent in the course of work or while acting as agent`
    ELSE
        IF `the employer proves reasonable steps`
        THEN `no: the employer or principal has a defence, having proved it took reasonable steps to prevent the contravention (s 133(2)); the worker or agent remains liable`
        ELSE `yes: the employer or principal and its worker or agent are jointly and severally liable, and you may complain against either or both (s 133(1))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Chapter 7, part 1, division 1 -- making a complaint`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 134(1): "Any of the following people may complain to the commissioner about an alleged contravention of the Act--
-- (a) a person who was subjected to the alleged contravention; (b) an agent of the person; (c) a person authorised in
-- writing by the commissioner to act on behalf of a person who was subjected to the alleged contravention and who is
-- unable to make or authorise a complaint." (3): "A relevant entity may complain to the commissioner about a relevant
-- alleged contravention" (a contravention of s 124A). (4): "the commissioner may accept the relevant entity's complaint
-- ... only if the commissioner is satisfied that-- (a) the complaint is made in good faith; and (b) the relevant alleged
-- contravention is about conduct that has affected or is likely to affect relevant persons for the relevant entity; and
-- (c) it is in the interests of justice to accept the complaint."
DECLARE `Who I am in the complaint` IS ONE OF
    `the person subjected to the alleged contravention`
    `an agent of that person`
    `a person authorised in writing by the commissioner to act for a person who was subjected to it and cannot make or authorise a complaint`
    `a body whose primary purpose is promoting the interests or welfare of people of a particular race, religion, sexuality, gender identity or sex characteristics`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Can I complain` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may complain to the Human Rights Commissioner (s 134(1))`
    `yes: the body may complain about the vilification, and the commissioner may accept the complaint (s 134(3), (4))`
    `no: the commissioner may accept the body's complaint only if satisfied it is made in good faith, about conduct that has affected or is likely to affect the people the body serves, and in the interests of justice (s 134(4))`
    `not covered: section 134(3) lets such a body complain only about vilification under s 124A`
    `not covered: section 134 does not name you among the people who may complain`

@export Can I make a complaint to the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 134? The person subjected to the alleged contravention may complain, or their agent, or a person the commissioner authorises in writing to act for someone who cannot complain; two or more people may complain jointly. A body promoting the interests of people of a particular race, religion, sexuality, gender identity or sex characteristics may complain about vilification.
GIVEN `who I am` IS A `Who I am in the complaint` @desc Who is making the complaint
      `it is about vilification` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint is about vilification under s 124A
      `the commissioner is satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a body's vilification complaint: whether the commissioner is satisfied that it is made in good faith, about conduct that has affected or is likely to affect the people the body serves, and that accepting it is in the interests of justice
GIVETH A `Can I complain`
`Can I make a complaint` `who I am` `it is about vilification` `the commissioner is satisfied` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who I am`
    WHEN `someone else` THEN `not covered: section 134 does not name you among the people who may complain`
    WHEN `a body whose primary purpose is promoting the interests or welfare of people of a particular race, religion, sexuality, gender identity or sex characteristics` THEN
        IF NOT (`it is about vilification`)
        THEN `not covered: section 134(3) lets such a body complain only about vilification under s 124A`
        ELSE
            IF `the commissioner is satisfied`
            THEN `yes: the body may complain about the vilification, and the commissioner may accept the complaint (s 134(3), (4))`
            ELSE `no: the commissioner may accept the body's complaint only if satisfied it is made in good faith, about conduct that has affected or is likely to affect the people the body serves, and in the interests of justice (s 134(4))`
    OTHERWISE `yes: you may complain to the Human Rights Commissioner (s 134(1))`

-- s 136: "A complaint must-- (a) be in writing; and (b) set out reasonably sufficient details to indicate an alleged
-- contravention of the Act; and (c) state the complainant's address for service; and (d) be lodged with, or sent by post
-- to, the commissioner." A bare "must": a complaint lacking one is answered "no" (fork F5).
@export Is my complaint in the form the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 136 requires? It must be in writing, set out reasonably sufficient details to indicate an alleged contravention, state your address for service, and be lodged with or posted to the Human Rights Commissioner. One complaint may allege more than one contravention (s 135).
GIVEN `it is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint is in writing
      `it sets out sufficient details` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it sets out reasonably sufficient details to indicate an alleged contravention of the Act
      `it states my address for service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it states your address for service (where documents can be given to you)
      `it is lodged or posted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is lodged with, or sent by post to, the Human Rights Commissioner
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my complaint in the proper form` `it is in writing` `it sets out sufficient details` `it states my address for service` `it is lodged or posted` IF
        `it is in writing`
    AND `it sets out sufficient details`
    AND `it states my address for service`
    AND `it is lodged or posted`

-- s 137(1): "The commissioner may accept a complaint from a person who had previously agreed with another person not to
-- complain, if the commissioner is of the reasonable opinion that it is fair to accept the complaint." (2): the
-- commissioner considers the parties' knowledge and what the person received in return. s 142(4): a rejection
-- "includes ... deciding not to accept a complaint under section 137(1)".
DECLARE `Does my agreement stop my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `no: the commissioner may accept your complaint despite the agreement, being of the reasonable opinion that it is fair to (s 137(1))`
    `yes: the commissioner is not of the opinion that accepting it is fair, and a decision not to accept it is a rejection: the complaint lapses and you cannot complain again about the same act or omission (ss 137(1), 142(1), (4))`
    `not covered: you made no agreement not to complain, so s 137 does not arise`

@export If I agreed not to complain (for example in a settlement or separation agreement), does that stop my complaint, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 137? The commissioner may still accept it if of the reasonable opinion that it is fair to, considering what each party knew and what you received in return.
GIVEN `I agreed not to complain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you previously agreed with another person not to complain
      `the commissioner considers it fair` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the commissioner is of the reasonable opinion that it is fair to accept your complaint, considering what the parties knew and what you received in return for the agreement
GIVETH A `Does my agreement stop my complaint`
`Does my agreement not to complain stop my complaint` `I agreed not to complain` `the commissioner considers it fair` MEANS
    IF NOT (`I agreed not to complain`)
    THEN `not covered: you made no agreement not to complain, so s 137 does not arise`
    ELSE
        IF `the commissioner considers it fair`
        THEN `no: the commissioner may accept your complaint despite the agreement, being of the reasonable opinion that it is fair to (s 137(1))`
        ELSE `yes: the commissioner is not of the opinion that accepting it is fair, and a decision not to accept it is a rejection: the complaint lapses and you cannot complain again about the same act or omission (ss 137(1), 142(1), (4))`

-- s 138(1): "Subject to subsection (2), a person is only entitled to make a complaint within 1 year of the alleged
-- contravention of the Act." "Within 1 year of" a day: the last day is the same day a year on (forks F1, F2).
GIVEN `the day of the contravention` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 138(1) -- the last day to complain` `the day of the contravention` MEANS
    `the date` 1 `years after` `the day of the contravention`

-- The rule: I DO make my complaint WITHIN the days to the last day. Not complaining is not a wrong; the BREACH marks only
-- that the year passed.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 138(1) -- making the complaint` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `make my complaint to the commissioner`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "more than 1 year has passed since the alleged contravention; the commissioner may accept the complaint only if satisfied you have shown good cause (s 138(2))"

GIVEN `the day of the contravention` IS A DATE, `the day I complain` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 138(1) -- complained within 1 year of` `the day of the contravention` `on` `the day I complain` IF
    `done in time under` (`s 138(1) -- making the complaint` (`the days from` `the day of the contravention` `to` (`s 138(1) -- the last day to complain` `the day of the contravention`))) `make my complaint to the commissioner` `at` (`the days from` `the day of the contravention` `to` `the day I complain`)

@export By when must I make my complaint to the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 138(1)? Within 1 year of the alleged contravention. A later complaint can be accepted only if the commissioner is satisfied you have shown good cause (s 138(2)).
GIVEN `the day of the contravention` IS A DATE @desc The day the alleged contravention happened (for a series, the day of the one you are asking about); ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I make my complaint` `the day of the contravention` MEANS
    `s 138(1) -- the last day to complain` `the day of the contravention`

-- s 138(2): "If a complaint is made more than 1 year after the alleged contravention of the Act, the commissioner must
-- decide-- (a) to accept the complaint, but only if the commissioner is satisfied the complainant has shown good cause;
-- or (b) otherwise--not to accept the complaint." s 141A: where a complaint alleges a within-time and an out-of-time
-- contravention, the commissioner may accept it for the within-time contravention and defer deciding about the
-- out-of-time one "until the commissioner has tried to resolve the complaint by conciliation". s 142(1), (4): not
-- accepting under s 138(2)(b) is a rejection, and a rejected complaint lapses and cannot be made again.
DECLARE `Is my complaint in time` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the complaint is made within 1 year of every contravention it alleges (s 138(1))`
    `late, but it may be accepted: the commissioner is satisfied you have shown good cause (s 138(2)(a))`
    `no: the complaint is made more than 1 year after the contravention and no good cause is shown, so it is not accepted; that is a rejection, and you cannot complain again about the same act or omission (ss 138(2)(b), 142(1), (4))`
    `partly: the contraventions within the year are in time; the earlier ones may also be accepted, as you have shown good cause, and the commissioner may defer that decision until after conciliation (ss 138(2)(a), 141A)`
    `partly: the contraventions within the year are in time; the earlier ones are not accepted, as no good cause is shown, though the commissioner may defer that decision until after conciliation (ss 138(2)(b), 141A, 142(4), (5))`

@export Is my complaint to the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner in time, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 138 and 141A? A complaint must be made within 1 year of the alleged contravention; a later one is accepted only if the commissioner is satisfied you have shown good cause. Where a complaint alleges contraventions both within and more than a year before, the commissioner may accept the recent ones and decide on the older ones after conciliation.
GIVEN `the day of the earliest contravention` IS A DATE @desc The day the earliest contravention your complaint alleges happened; ISO 8601
      `the day of the latest contravention` IS A DATE @desc The day the latest contravention your complaint alleges happened (the same day if there is only one); ISO 8601
      `the day I complain` IS A DATE @desc The day you make (made) the complaint to the commissioner; ISO 8601
      `the commissioner is satisfied I have shown good cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a late complaint: whether the commissioner is satisfied you have shown good cause for making it more than 1 year after the contravention
GIVETH AN `Is my complaint in time`
`Is my complaint in time` `the day of the earliest contravention` `the day of the latest contravention` `the day I complain` `the commissioner is satisfied I have shown good cause` MEANS
    IF `s 138(1) -- complained within 1 year of` `the day of the earliest contravention` `on` `the day I complain`
    THEN `yes: the complaint is made within 1 year of every contravention it alleges (s 138(1))`
    ELSE
        IF `s 138(1) -- complained within 1 year of` `the day of the latest contravention` `on` `the day I complain`
        THEN
            IF `the commissioner is satisfied I have shown good cause`
            THEN `partly: the contraventions within the year are in time; the earlier ones may also be accepted, as you have shown good cause, and the commissioner may defer that decision until after conciliation (ss 138(2)(a), 141A)`
            ELSE `partly: the contraventions within the year are in time; the earlier ones are not accepted, as no good cause is shown, though the commissioner may defer that decision until after conciliation (ss 138(2)(b), 141A, 142(4), (5))`
        ELSE
            IF `the commissioner is satisfied I have shown good cause`
            THEN `late, but it may be accepted: the commissioner is satisfied you have shown good cause (s 138(2)(a))`
            ELSE `no: the complaint is made more than 1 year after the contravention and no good cause is shown, so it is not accepted; that is a rejection, and you cannot complain again about the same act or omission (ss 138(2)(b), 142(1), (4))`

-- s 139: "The commissioner must reject a complaint if the commissioner is of the reasonable opinion that the complaint
-- is-- (a) frivolous, trivial or vexatious; or (b) misconceived or lacking in substance." s 140(1): "The commissioner
-- may reject or stay a complaint if-- (a) there are concurrent proceedings in a court or tribunal in relation to the act
-- or omission ...; or (b) the commissioner reasonably considers the act or omission ... may be effectively or
-- conveniently dealt with by another entity." (2): "may also reject a complaint if the commissioner reasonably
-- considers the act or omission ... has been adequately dealt with by another entity." (3): time limits do not run
-- while a complaint is stayed. s 142(1): a rejected complaint lapses and cannot be made again.
DECLARE `Can my complaint be rejected` IS ONE OF
    `yes, it must be: the commissioner is of the reasonable opinion that it is frivolous, trivial, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance (s 139); it lapses and you cannot complain again about the same act or omission (s 142(1))`
    `it may be rejected or stayed: there are concurrent proceedings in a court or tribunal, or another entity may deal with it effectively or conveniently (s 140(1)); time limits do not run while it is stayed (s 140(3)), and a rejected complaint lapses (s 142(1))`
    `it may be rejected: another entity has adequately dealt with the act or omission (s 140(2)); a rejected complaint lapses (s 142(1))`
    `no: neither s 139 nor s 140 gives a ground to reject it`

@export Can my complaint be rejected by the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 139, 140 and 142? It must be rejected if the commissioner reasonably considers it frivolous, trivial, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance; it may be rejected or stayed if there are proceedings elsewhere or another body can deal with it, or has adequately dealt with it. A rejected complaint lapses and cannot be made again. (A late complaint and an agreement not to complain are separate questions.)
GIVEN `the commissioner thinks it frivolous or lacking in substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the commissioner is of the reasonable opinion that the complaint is frivolous, trivial or vexatious, or misconceived or lacking in substance
      `there are concurrent proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there are proceedings in a court or tribunal about the same act or omission
      `another entity may deal with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the commissioner reasonably considers the act or omission may be effectively or conveniently dealt with by another entity
      `another entity has adequately dealt with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the commissioner reasonably considers the act or omission has been adequately dealt with by another entity
GIVETH A `Can my complaint be rejected`
`Can my complaint be rejected` `the commissioner thinks it frivolous or lacking in substance` `there are concurrent proceedings` `another entity may deal with it` `another entity has adequately dealt with it` MEANS
    IF `the commissioner thinks it frivolous or lacking in substance`
    THEN `yes, it must be: the commissioner is of the reasonable opinion that it is frivolous, trivial, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance (s 139); it lapses and you cannot complain again about the same act or omission (s 142(1))`
    ELSE
        IF `there are concurrent proceedings` OR `another entity may deal with it`
        THEN `it may be rejected or stayed: there are concurrent proceedings in a court or tribunal, or another entity may deal with it effectively or conveniently (s 140(1)); time limits do not run while it is stayed (s 140(3)), and a rejected complaint lapses (s 142(1))`
        ELSE
            IF `another entity has adequately dealt with it`
            THEN `it may be rejected: another entity has adequately dealt with the act or omission (s 140(2)); a rejected complaint lapses (s 142(1))`
            ELSE `no: neither s 139 nor s 140 gives a ground to reject it`

-- s 141(1): "The commissioner must decide whether to accept or reject a complaint within 28 days of receiving the
-- complaint." (2): and "must promptly notify the complainant". (3): subject to s 141A.
@export By when must the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner decide whether to accept or reject my complaint, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 141? Within 28 days of receiving it, and the commissioner must then promptly tell you. (The decision on an out-of-time part may be deferred until after conciliation, s 141A.)
GIVEN `the day the commissioner received it` IS A DATE @desc The day the commissioner received your complaint; ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the commissioner decide whether to accept my complaint` `the day the commissioner received it` MEANS
    `the day` 28 `days after` `the day the commissioner received it`

-- s 142(2): "If a complaint is rejected, the complainant may, within 28 days of receiving notice of the rejection, ask
-- the commissioner for written reasons." (3): the commissioner "must promptly give" them.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 142(2) -- asking for reasons` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `ask the commissioner for written reasons for the rejection`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the 28 days after receiving notice of the rejection have passed without a request for written reasons"

@export By when can I ask the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner for written reasons for rejecting my complaint, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 142(2)? Within 28 days of receiving notice of the rejection; the commissioner must then promptly give them.
GIVEN `the day I received notice of the rejection` IS A DATE @desc The day you received notice that your complaint was rejected (or not accepted); ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when can I ask for reasons for the rejection` `the day I received notice of the rejection` MEANS
    `the day` 28 `days after` `the day I received notice of the rejection`

@export Am I in time to ask the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner for written reasons for rejecting my complaint, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 142(2), (3)? If you ask within 28 days of receiving notice of the rejection, the commissioner must promptly give you written reasons.
GIVEN `the day I received notice of the rejection` IS A DATE @desc The day you received notice that your complaint was rejected (or not accepted); ISO 8601
      `the day I ask` IS A DATE @desc The day you ask (or asked) the commissioner for written reasons; ISO 8601
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I in time to ask for reasons for the rejection` `the day I received notice of the rejection` `the day I ask` IF
    `done in time under` (`s 142(2) -- asking for reasons` 28) `ask the commissioner for written reasons for the rejection` `at` (`the days from` `the day I received notice of the rejection` `to` `the day I ask`)

-- s 153: "If, but for this section-- (a) a worker is dismissed in circumstances entitling the worker to-- (i) lodge a
-- complaint with the commissioner under the Act; and (ii) apply for industrial relief; and (b) the worker does not apply
-- for industrial relief before lodging a complaint and being notified under section 141 that the complaint has been
-- accepted; the worker may only proceed with the complaint and may not later apply for industrial relief in respect of
-- the circumstances." s 154: if the worker "applies for industrial relief before lodging a complaint; the worker may
-- proceed with both the complaint and the application for industrial relief, but the tribunal may not make an order in
-- respect of the complaint requiring the reinstatement or re-employment of the worker."
DECLARE `What I did first` IS ONE OF
    `I applied for industrial relief before lodging my complaint`
    `I lodged my complaint and was notified it was accepted, without having applied for industrial relief`
    `I lodged my complaint, then applied for industrial relief before being notified it was accepted`
    `I have done neither yet`

DECLARE `If I was dismissed` IS ONE OF
    `you may proceed with both, but the tribunal cannot order your reinstatement or re-employment on the complaint (s 154)`
    `you may only proceed with the complaint, and cannot later apply for industrial relief for the same circumstances (s 153)`
    `not covered: neither s 153 nor s 154 applies where industrial relief is sought after the complaint is lodged but before notice that it was accepted`
    `you choose: applying for industrial relief first keeps both, but the tribunal then cannot order reinstatement or re-employment (s 154); lodging the complaint and being notified it is accepted first loses industrial relief (s 153)`
    `not covered: sections 153 and 154 apply only to a worker dismissed in circumstances giving both a complaint and industrial relief`

@export I was dismissed: can I both complain under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 and apply for industrial relief (unfair dismissal relief under the Industrial Relations Act 2016), under ss 153 and 154? It depends on which you do first: industrial relief first keeps both but rules out reinstatement through the discrimination complaint; a complaint accepted first rules out industrial relief.
GIVEN `I can do both` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were dismissed in circumstances entitling you both to complain under the Anti-Discrimination Act and to apply for industrial relief under the Industrial Relations Act 2016, chapter 8, part 2
      `what I did first` IS A `What I did first` @desc What you have done so far, and in what order
GIVETH AN `If I was dismissed`
`If I was dismissed, can I complain and seek industrial relief` `I can do both` `what I did first` MEANS
    IF NOT (`I can do both`)
    THEN `not covered: sections 153 and 154 apply only to a worker dismissed in circumstances giving both a complaint and industrial relief`
    ELSE
        CONSIDER `what I did first`
        WHEN `I applied for industrial relief before lodging my complaint` THEN `you may proceed with both, but the tribunal cannot order your reinstatement or re-employment on the complaint (s 154)`
        WHEN `I lodged my complaint and was notified it was accepted, without having applied for industrial relief` THEN `you may only proceed with the complaint, and cannot later apply for industrial relief for the same circumstances (s 153)`
        WHEN `I lodged my complaint, then applied for industrial relief before being notified it was accepted` THEN `not covered: neither s 153 nor s 154 applies where industrial relief is sought after the complaint is lodged but before notice that it was accepted`
        OTHERWISE `you choose: applying for industrial relief first keeps both, but the tribunal then cannot order reinstatement or re-employment (s 154); lodging the complaint and being notified it is accepted first loses industrial relief (s 153)`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Chapter 7, part 1, divisions 3 and 4 -- conciliation, and getting the complaint to the tribunal`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- Schedule 1: "tribunal means-- (a) in relation to a work-related matter--the industrial relations commission; or (b) in
-- relation to any other matter--QCAT." "work-related matter means a complaint or other matter relating to, or including,
-- work or the work-related area." ss 164A(2), 166(1), 167(1): a complaint that "is or includes a work-related matter" is
-- referred to the industrial relations commission (which may transfer a non-work part to QCAT, s 193A).
DECLARE `Which tribunal` IS ONE OF
    `the Queensland Industrial Relations Commission: the complaint is or includes a work-related matter (schedule 1; ss 164A(2)(a), 166(1)(a), 167(1)(a))`
    `the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal (QCAT): the complaint is not a work-related matter (schedule 1; ss 164A(2)(b), 166(1)(b), 167(1)(b))`

@export Which tribunal will hear my complaint if it is referred, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 (schedule 1, ss 164A, 166, 167)? A complaint that relates to or includes work or the work-related area goes to the Queensland Industrial Relations Commission; any other complaint goes to QCAT.
GIVEN `it relates to work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your complaint relates to, or includes, work or the work-related area (seeking work, work, partnerships, industrial and professional organisations, qualifications, employment agencies)
GIVETH A `Which tribunal`
`Which tribunal will hear my complaint` `it relates to work` MEANS
    IF `it relates to work`
    THEN `the Queensland Industrial Relations Commission: the complaint is or includes a work-related matter (schedule 1; ss 164A(2)(a), 166(1)(a), 167(1)(a))`
    ELSE `the Queensland Civil and Administrative Tribunal (QCAT): the complaint is not a work-related matter (schedule 1; ss 164A(2)(b), 166(1)(b), 167(1)(b))`

-- s 160(1): "If a complainant, without reasonable excuse, does not comply with a direction to attend a conciliation
-- conference, the commissioner may dismiss the complaint and direct the complainant to pay costs to the respondent."
-- (3), (4): a costs direction is enforceable by filing it with a court.
DECLARE `If I miss the conference` IS ONE OF
    `the commissioner may dismiss your complaint and direct you to pay the respondent's costs, which the respondent can enforce through a court (s 160(1), (3), (4))`
    `no: section 160(1) applies only if you, without reasonable excuse, do not comply with a direction to attend`

@export What can happen if I do not attend a conciliation conference I was directed to attend, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 160? Without a reasonable excuse, the commissioner may dismiss your complaint and direct you to pay the respondent's costs. (If the respondent stays away without excuse, it may be directed to pay your costs.)
GIVEN `I was directed to attend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the commissioner directed you to take part in a conciliation conference (a notice to the respondent can also take effect as such a direction, s 143(3))
      `I attended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you attended the conference
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse for not attending
GIVETH AN `If I miss the conference`
`What can happen if I miss the conciliation conference` `I was directed to attend` `I attended` `I had a reasonable excuse` MEANS
    IF `I was directed to attend` AND (NOT `I attended`) AND (NOT `I had a reasonable excuse`)
    THEN `the commissioner may dismiss your complaint and direct you to pay the respondent's costs, which the respondent can enforce through a court (s 160(1), (3), (4))`
    ELSE `no: section 160(1) applies only if you, without reasonable excuse, do not comply with a direction to attend`

-- s 164: "(1) If the complaint is resolved by conciliation, the commissioner must record the terms of the agreement and
-- have the document signed by the complainant and the respondent. (2) The commissioner must provide a copy of the
-- document to each party and file the document with the tribunal. (3) The agreement is then enforceable as if it were an
-- order of the tribunal." s 189: the same for an agreement reached after referral and before the tribunal decides,
-- recorded in a document the parties sign and file with the tribunal.
@export Is the agreement settling my complaint enforceable as if it were an order of the tribunal, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 164 and 189? Yes, once its terms are recorded in a document signed by you and the respondent and the document is filed with the tribunal.
GIVEN `the terms are recorded and signed by both of us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the terms of the agreement are recorded in a document signed by you and the respondent
      `the document is filed with the tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document has been filed with the tribunal (by the commissioner after conciliation, or by the parties after referral)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is our settlement enforceable as a tribunal order` `the terms are recorded and signed by both of us` `the document is filed with the tribunal` IF
        `the terms are recorded and signed by both of us`
    AND `the document is filed with the tribunal`

-- s 164A(1)-(3): if a conciliation conference has been held and the complaint is not resolved, "The complainant may give
-- the commissioner a written notice requiring the commissioner to refer the complaint", and the commissioner "must
-- promptly" refer it; (4): not if the commissioner acts under s 168. s 165(3): "If the commissioner gives notice under
-- subsection (1) [that the complaint cannot be resolved by conciliation], sections 164A and 167 stop applying".
DECLARE `Can I require referral after the conference` IS ONE OF
    `yes: by written notice to the commissioner, at any time; the commissioner must promptly refer it to the tribunal, unless the commissioner acts under s 168 because the complaint is frivolous or lacking in substance (s 164A(2) to (4))`
    `no: once the commissioner has given notice that the complaint cannot be resolved by conciliation, s 164A stops applying; you may require referral under s 166 within 28 days of that notice (s 165(3))`
    `no: the complaint was resolved by conciliation; the agreement is enforceable once recorded, signed and filed (s 164)`
    `no: section 164A applies only after a conciliation conference has been held`

@export After a conciliation conference that did not resolve my complaint, can I require the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner to refer it to the tribunal, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 164A and 165? Yes, by written notice, and the commissioner must promptly refer it, unless the commissioner has instead given notice that it cannot be resolved by conciliation (then s 166 and its 28 days apply).
GIVEN `a conference has been held` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a conciliation conference has been held about your complaint
      `it was resolved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint was resolved by conciliation
      `I was told it cannot be conciliated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the commissioner has given you written notice that the complaint cannot be resolved by conciliation (s 165)
GIVETH A `Can I require referral after the conference`
`Can I require referral after the conciliation conference` `a conference has been held` `it was resolved` `I was told it cannot be conciliated` MEANS
    IF `I was told it cannot be conciliated`
    THEN `no: once the commissioner has given notice that the complaint cannot be resolved by conciliation, s 164A stops applying; you may require referral under s 166 within 28 days of that notice (s 165(3))`
    ELSE
        IF NOT (`a conference has been held`)
        THEN `no: section 164A applies only after a conciliation conference has been held`
        ELSE
            IF `it was resolved`
            THEN `no: the complaint was resolved by conciliation; the agreement is enforceable once recorded, signed and filed (s 164)`
            ELSE `yes: by written notice to the commissioner, at any time; the commissioner must promptly refer it to the tribunal, unless the commissioner acts under s 168 because the complaint is frivolous or lacking in substance (s 164A(2) to (4))`

-- s 166(1): "Within 28 days of being notified that the complaint can not be resolved by conciliation, a complainant
-- may, by written notice, require the commissioner to refer the complaint". (2): the commissioner may extend the time if
-- the complainant asks in writing "within the 28 days". (3): "the day the complainant asks for the extension, the day the
-- complainant is given written notice of the commissioner's decision about the extension and any period between those
-- days, is not included in the period". (4): "if the complainant asks for the extension on the last day of the period
-- ... and the extension is subsequently refused, the complainant may require the commissioner to refer the complaint
-- ... by making a written request on the day the complainant receives written notice of the refusal or on the next day
-- that is a business day."
@export By when must I require the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner to refer my complaint to the tribunal after being told it cannot be resolved by conciliation, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 166? Within 28 days of being notified. If you ask in writing within the 28 days for an extension, the days from your request to the notice of the decision do not count; if the extension is granted, the commissioner sets the new time; if you asked on the last day and it is refused, you have until the next business day after the refusal.
GIVEN `the day I was notified` IS A DATE @desc The day you were notified that the complaint cannot be resolved by conciliation; ISO 8601
      `I asked for an extension` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the commissioner in writing for an extension of the 28 days
      `the day I asked` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked for the extension (any date if you did not); ISO 8601
      `the day I was told the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given written notice of the commissioner's decision on the extension (any date if you did not ask); ISO 8601
      `it was granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the commissioner granted the extension
      `the extended last day` IS A DATE @desc If the extension was granted: the last day the commissioner allowed (any date otherwise); ISO 8601
      `the next business day after the refusal` IS A DATE @desc If the extension was refused: the next business day after the day you received written notice of the refusal (any date otherwise); ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I require referral after the conciliation notice` `the day I was notified` `I asked for an extension` `the day I asked` `the day I was told the decision` `it was granted` `the extended last day` `the next business day after the refusal` MEANS
    IF NOT (`I asked for an extension` AND (`the day` `the day I asked` `is on or before` `the 28th day`))
    THEN `the 28th day`
    ELSE
        IF `it was granted`
        THEN `the extended last day`
        ELSE
            IF DATE_SERIAL `the day I asked` EQUALS DATE_SERIAL `the 28th day`
            THEN `the later of` `the day pushed out` `and` `the next business day after the refusal`
            ELSE `the day pushed out`
    WHERE
        `the 28th day` MEANS `the day` 28 `days after` `the day I was notified`
        `the days not counted` MEANS (`the days from` `the day I asked` `to` `the day I was told the decision`) PLUS 1
        `the day pushed out` MEANS `the day` `the days not counted` `days after` `the 28th day`

-- s 167(1): "If the commissioner has not finished dealing with a complaint 6 months after informing the complainant and
-- the respondent that the complaint has been accepted, either the complainant or the respondent may, by written notice,
-- request the commissioner to refer the complaint". (2): the commissioner may defer acting "for up to 28 days, if there
-- is a significant prospect that the matter can be resolved by conciliation within that period". (5): "If the
-- complainant requests the commissioner in writing, to refer the complaint, the commissioner must comply." The 6 months
-- end on the same day of the month (fork F2); s 167 stops applying once a s 164A notice or a s 165 notice is given
-- (ss 164A(6), 165(3)).
@export From what day can I ask the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner to refer my complaint to the tribunal because it has not been finished, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 167? Once 6 months have passed since you were told the complaint was accepted, if the commissioner has not finished with it; on your written request the commissioner must refer it, though it may wait up to 28 days if conciliation looks likely to succeed. This route closes once you are told the complaint cannot be conciliated, or you require referral after a conference.
GIVEN `the day I was told it was accepted` IS A DATE @desc The day the commissioner informed you (and the respondent) that the complaint was accepted; ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`From when can I ask for referral because the complaint is taking too long` `the day I was told it was accepted` MEANS
    `the same day` 6 `months after` `the day I was told it was accepted`

-- s 167(4): if the respondent requests referral, "(a) the commissioner must ask the complainant whether the complainant
-- agrees to the complaint being referred; and (b) if the complainant agrees in writing--the commissioner must refer the
-- complaint to the tribunal; and (c) if the complainant does not agree in writing within 28 days--the complaint lapses,
-- and the complainant can not make a further complaint relating to the act or omission that was the subject of the
-- complaint; and (d) the commissioner may extend the 28 day period, but only if the complainant asks, in writing, for an
-- extension before the 28 days have passed." The rule: I MUST agree in writing WITHIN 28 days LEST the complaint lapse.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 167(4) -- agreeing to the respondent's referral` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    MUST `agree in writing to the complaint being referred`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "you did not agree in writing in time, so the complaint lapses and you cannot make a further complaint about the same act or omission (s 167(4)(c))"

DECLARE `If the respondent asks for referral` IS ONE OF
    `the complaint is referred: you agreed in writing in time, so the commissioner must refer it to the tribunal (s 167(4)(b))`
    `the complaint lapses: you did not agree in writing in time, and you cannot make a further complaint about the same act or omission (s 167(4)(c))`

@export If the respondent asks for my complaint to be referred to the tribunal after 6 months, what happens if I do or do not agree, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 167(4)? If you agree in writing within 28 days of the commissioner asking you, it is referred; if not, the complaint lapses and you cannot complain again about the same act. The commissioner may extend the 28 days only if you ask in writing before they pass.
GIVEN `the day the commissioner asked me` IS A DATE @desc The day the commissioner asked whether you agree to the complaint being referred; ISO 8601
      `I agreed in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you agreed in writing to the referral
      `the day I agreed` IS A DATE @desc The day you agreed in writing (any date if you did not); ISO 8601
      `I asked for more time before the 28 days passed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked the commissioner in writing for an extension before the 28 days had passed
      `the commissioner extended the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the commissioner extended the 28 days
      `the extended last day` IS A DATE @desc If the commissioner extended the time: the last day allowed (any date otherwise); ISO 8601
GIVETH AN `If the respondent asks for referral`
`If the respondent asks for referral, does my complaint lapse` `the day the commissioner asked me` `I agreed in writing` `the day I agreed` `I asked for more time before the 28 days passed` `the commissioner extended the time` `the extended last day` MEANS
    IF     `I agreed in writing`
       AND (`done in time under` (`s 167(4) -- agreeing to the respondent's referral` `the days allowed`) `agree in writing to the complaint being referred` `at` (`the days from` `the day the commissioner asked me` `to` `the day I agreed`))
    THEN `the complaint is referred: you agreed in writing in time, so the commissioner must refer it to the tribunal (s 167(4)(b))`
    ELSE `the complaint lapses: you did not agree in writing in time, and you cannot make a further complaint about the same act or omission (s 167(4)(c))`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS
            IF `I asked for more time before the 28 days passed` AND `the commissioner extended the time`
            THEN `the days from` `the day the commissioner asked me` `to` `the extended last day`
            ELSE 28

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Chapter 7, part 1, division 5 -- lapse and withdrawal (situation: the commissioner has written to say the complaint may lapse)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 168(2), (3): a notice that "unless the complainant is able to show to the commissioner's satisfaction within 28 days
-- that the complaint is not frivolous, trivial, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance-- (a) the complaint will
-- lapse"; it lapses if "at the end of 28 days" the commissioner is still of that opinion. s 168A(3)(d), (4): a show
-- cause notice; the complainant "may, within 28 days after receipt of the notice, make written representations"; the
-- complaint lapses if, "after considering any submissions made within the show cause period", the commissioner
-- reasonably considers another entity has adequately dealt, or may more effectively or conveniently deal, with it.
-- s 169(1), (2): a notice that the complaint "will lapse unless the complainant indicates that the complainant wishes to
-- continue with it"; "If the complainant does not give the indication within 28 days, the complaint lapses". s 170(2),
-- (4): after the complainant's written notice of not wanting to continue, the complaint lapses "at the end of 28 days
-- after the notice is given ... unless the complainant gives ... a continuation notice"; "(a) the complaint lapses on
-- the day after the relevant period ends". In each, a lapsed complaint cannot be made again.
DECLARE `The commissioner's notice` IS ONE OF
    `a notice that my complaint appears frivolous, trivial, vexatious, misconceived or lacking in substance (s 168)`
    `a show cause notice that another entity has dealt, or may better deal, with the complaint (s 168A)`
    `a notice that I appear to have lost interest in the complaint (s 169)`
    `a notice given after I said I do not want to continue with the complaint (s 170)`

@export By when must I answer the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner's notice that my complaint may lapse, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 168, 168A, 169 and 170? Within 28 days of the notice: to show the complaint is not frivolous or lacking in substance (s 168), to make written representations (s 168A), to say you wish to continue (s 169), or to give a continuation notice after asking to withdraw (s 170).
GIVEN `the notice` IS A `The commissioner's notice` @desc Which notice the commissioner gave you
      `the day I received the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you received (were given) the notice; ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I answer the lapse notice` `the notice` `the day I received the notice` MEANS
    `the day` 28 `days after` `the day I received the notice`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`ss 169, 170 -- answering the notice` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `answer the commissioner's notice`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the 28 days have passed without your answer, so the complaint lapses and cannot be made again (ss 169(2), 170(4))"

DECLARE `Does my complaint lapse` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the complaint lapses, and you cannot make a further complaint about the same act or omission`
    `no: the complaint does not lapse under this section, and the commissioner deals with it`

@export Does my complaint lapse after the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner's notice, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 168 to 170? For a notice that you seem to have lost interest (s 169), or one given after you asked to withdraw (s 170), it lapses unless you answer within 28 days. For a notice that it seems frivolous (s 168) or a show cause notice (s 168A), it lapses if, after the 28 days and your answer, the commissioner still holds that view. A lapsed complaint cannot be made again.
GIVEN `the notice` IS A `The commissioner's notice` @desc Which notice the commissioner gave you
      `the day I received the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you received (were given) the notice; ISO 8601
      `I answered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you answered: said you wish to continue (s 169), gave a continuation notice (s 170), or made representations or showed the complaint has substance (ss 168, 168A)
      `the day I answered` IS A DATE @desc The day you answered (any date if you did not); ISO 8601
      `the commissioner still holds that view` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a s 168 or s 168A notice: whether, at the end of the 28 days (after any representations made in time), the commissioner is still of the reasonable opinion the notice gave
GIVETH A `Does my complaint lapse`
`Does my complaint lapse after the notice` `the notice` `the day I received the notice` `I answered` `the day I answered` `the commissioner still holds that view` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the notice`
    WHEN `a notice that I appear to have lost interest in the complaint (s 169)` THEN `by my answer`
    WHEN `a notice given after I said I do not want to continue with the complaint (s 170)` THEN `by my answer`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `the commissioner still holds that view`
        THEN `yes: the complaint lapses, and you cannot make a further complaint about the same act or omission`
        ELSE `no: the complaint does not lapse under this section, and the commissioner deals with it`
    WHERE
        `by my answer` MEANS
            IF     `I answered`
               AND (`done in time under` (`ss 169, 170 -- answering the notice` 28) `answer the commissioner's notice` `at` (`the days from` `the day I received the notice` `to` `the day I answered`))
            THEN `no: the complaint does not lapse under this section, and the commissioner deals with it`
            ELSE `yes: the complaint lapses, and you cannot make a further complaint about the same act or omission`

@export If I told the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner I do not want to continue, on what day does my complaint lapse if I give no continuation notice, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 170(4)? On the day after the 28 days after the commissioner's notice to you end.
GIVEN `the day the commissioner's notice was given` IS A DATE @desc The day the commissioner gave you the s 170(2) notice; ISO 8601
GIVETH A DATE
`On what day does my withdrawn complaint lapse` `the day the commissioner's notice was given` MEANS
    `the day` 29 `days after` `the day the commissioner's notice was given`

-- s 169(3): "Within 28 days of being notified that the complaint has lapsed, the complainant may apply to the tribunal to
-- review the commissioner's decision." (4): "If the tribunal is satisfied that the complainant has a genuine interest in
-- continuing with the complaint, the commissioner must resume dealing with the complaint."
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 169(3) -- applying for review of the lapse` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `apply to the tribunal to review the lapse`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the 28 days after notice of the lapse have passed without an application for review"

DECLARE `Can I have the lapse reviewed` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you applied in time; if the tribunal is satisfied you have a genuine interest in continuing, the commissioner must resume dealing with the complaint (s 169(3), (4))`
    `no: the 28 days after notice of the lapse have passed (s 169(3))`

@export My complaint lapsed because the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner thought I had lost interest: can I have that reviewed by the tribunal, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 169(3), (4)? Yes, if you apply within 28 days of being notified of the lapse; if the tribunal is satisfied you have a genuine interest in continuing, the commissioner must resume.
GIVEN `the day I was notified of the lapse` IS A DATE @desc The day you were notified that the complaint lapsed; ISO 8601
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply (or applied) to the tribunal for review; ISO 8601
GIVETH A `Can I have the lapse reviewed`
`Can I have the lapse reviewed` `the day I was notified of the lapse` `the day I apply` MEANS
    IF `done in time under` (`s 169(3) -- applying for review of the lapse` 28) `apply to the tribunal to review the lapse` `at` (`the days from` `the day I was notified of the lapse` `to` `the day I apply`)
    THEN `yes: you applied in time; if the tribunal is satisfied you have a genuine interest in continuing, the commissioner must resume dealing with the complaint (s 169(3), (4))`
    ELSE `no: the 28 days after notice of the lapse have passed (s 169(3))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Chapter 7, part 2 -- before the tribunal (situation: the complaint has been referred)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 144(1): "At any time before a complaint is referred to the tribunal, the complainant or the commissioner may apply
-- ... to the tribunal for an order prohibiting a person from doing an act that might prejudice-- (a) the investigation or
-- conciliation of the complaint; or (b) an order that the tribunal might make after a hearing." (3): it may be heard in
-- the respondent's absence if the tribunal is satisfied it is in the interests of justice. s 190(1), (2): "This section
-- applies if a complaint has been referred to, but not yet determined by, the tribunal. ... The complainant may apply to
-- the tribunal for an order prohibiting a person from doing an act that might prejudice an order the tribunal might make
-- after a hearing."
DECLARE `Where my complaint is` IS ONE OF
    `with the commissioner, not yet referred`
    `referred to the tribunal, not yet decided`
    `decided by the tribunal`

DECLARE `Can I get a protecting order` IS ONE OF
    `yes: before referral you may apply to the tribunal for an order stopping a person from doing an act that might prejudice the investigation, the conciliation or a later order; it may be heard without the respondent if the interests of justice require (s 144(1), (3))`
    `yes: after referral and before the decision you may apply for an order stopping a person from doing an act that might prejudice an order the tribunal might make (s 190(2))`
    `no: sections 144 and 190 apply only until the tribunal decides the complaint`

@export Can I ask the tribunal for an order to stop someone from doing something that might prejudice my complaint, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 144 and 190? Yes, at any time before referral (to protect the investigation, the conciliation or a later order) and after referral until the tribunal decides (to protect a later order).
GIVEN `where it is` IS A `Where my complaint is` @desc Where your complaint is: with the commissioner and not yet referred; referred to the tribunal and not yet decided; or decided by the tribunal
GIVETH A `Can I get a protecting order`
`Can I ask for an order protecting my complaint` `where it is` MEANS
    CONSIDER `where it is`
    WHEN `with the commissioner, not yet referred` THEN `yes: before referral you may apply to the tribunal for an order stopping a person from doing an act that might prejudice the investigation, the conciliation or a later order; it may be heard without the respondent if the interests of justice require (s 144(1), (3))`
    WHEN `referred to the tribunal, not yet decided` THEN `yes: after referral and before the decision you may apply for an order stopping a person from doing an act that might prejudice an order the tribunal might make (s 190(2))`
    OTHERWISE `no: sections 144 and 190 apply only until the tribunal decides the complaint`

-- s 175: "(1) The tribunal must accept a complaint that is referred to it by the commissioner, unless the complaint was
-- made to the commissioner more than 1 year after the alleged contravention of the Act. (2) If the complaint was made
-- more than 1 year after the alleged contravention, the tribunal may deal with the complaint if the tribunal considers
-- that, on the balance of fairness between the parties, it would be reasonable to do so."
DECLARE `Will the tribunal deal with it` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the tribunal must accept it, as the complaint was made within 1 year of the alleged contravention (s 175(1))`
    `yes: the complaint was made more than 1 year after the contravention, but the tribunal considers it reasonable, on the balance of fairness between the parties, to deal with it (s 175(2))`
    `no: the complaint was made more than 1 year after the contravention, and the tribunal does not consider it reasonable, on the balance of fairness, to deal with it (s 175(2))`

@export Will the tribunal deal with my complaint once the Queensland Human Rights Commissioner refers it, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 s 175? It must accept a complaint made within 1 year of the alleged contravention; a later one only if it considers it reasonable, on the balance of fairness between the parties.
GIVEN `the day of the contravention` IS A DATE @desc The day the alleged contravention happened; ISO 8601
      `the day I complained` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the complaint to the commissioner; ISO 8601
      `the tribunal considers it fair` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a complaint made more than 1 year after: whether the tribunal considers that, on the balance of fairness between the parties, it would be reasonable to deal with it
GIVETH A `Will the tribunal deal with it`
`Will the tribunal deal with my complaint` `the day of the contravention` `the day I complained` `the tribunal considers it fair` MEANS
    IF `s 138(1) -- complained within 1 year of` `the day of the contravention` `on` `the day I complained`
    THEN `yes: the tribunal must accept it, as the complaint was made within 1 year of the alleged contravention (s 175(1))`
    ELSE
        IF `the tribunal considers it fair`
        THEN `yes: the complaint was made more than 1 year after the contravention, but the tribunal considers it reasonable, on the balance of fairness between the parties, to deal with it (s 175(2))`
        ELSE `no: the complaint was made more than 1 year after the contravention, and the tribunal does not consider it reasonable, on the balance of fairness, to deal with it (s 175(2))`

-- s 204: "(1) In a complaint proceeding, if there are facts from which it could be decided, in the absence of any other
-- explanation, that the respondent contravened the provision of the Act the subject of the alleged contravention, the
-- respondent is taken to have contravened the provision. (2) Subsection (1) does not apply if the respondent proves, on
-- the balance of probabilities, that the respondent did not contravene the provision." s 205(1): "An exemption is a
-- defence to discrimination, and the person seeking to rely on the exemption has the onus of proving, on the balance of
-- probabilities, that the exemption applies." s 285AA: new ss 204 and 205 do not apply to a complaint made before "the
-- relevant commencement" and not finally dealt with, or to a complaint about a contravention that happened before it;
-- "Former sections 204 to 206 continue to apply". The relevant commencement (of s 7B of the Respect at Work and Other
-- Matters Amendment Act 2024) is not stated in the deposited text, so it is asked (finding 6).
DECLARE `Who must prove what` IS ONE OF
    `the former sections 204 to 206 apply, as in force before 1 December 2024 (s 285AA); their text is not in the deposited version, so this encoding cannot say more`
    `the respondent is taken to have contravened the Act: there are facts from which that could be decided, absent any other explanation, and the respondent has not proved it did not (s 204(1), (2)); any exemption is for the respondent to prove (s 205)`
    `the respondent proved, on the balance of probabilities, that it did not contravene the Act (s 204(2))`
    `section 204(1) does not help you: there are no facts from which a contravention could be decided absent any other explanation`

@export At the hearing of my complaint, who must prove what, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 204, 205 and 285AA? Under the current rule, if there are facts from which the tribunal could decide, absent any other explanation, that the respondent contravened the Act, the respondent is taken to have contravened it unless it proves it did not; the respondent must also prove any exemption. For a contravention before the new rule commenced, the former rules apply.
GIVEN `it happened before the new rule commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the alleged contravention happened before the commencement of section 7B of the Respect at Work and Other Matters Amendment Act 2024 (the relevant commencement in s 285AA), or the complaint was made before then and not finally dealt with
      `there are facts from which it could be decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there are facts from which the tribunal could decide, in the absence of any other explanation, that the respondent contravened the provision
      `the respondent proves it did not` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent proves, on the balance of probabilities, that it did not contravene the provision
GIVETH A `Who must prove what`
`Who must prove what at the hearing` `it happened before the new rule commenced` `there are facts from which it could be decided` `the respondent proves it did not` MEANS
    IF `it happened before the new rule commenced`
    THEN `the former sections 204 to 206 apply, as in force before 1 December 2024 (s 285AA); their text is not in the deposited version, so this encoding cannot say more`
    ELSE
        IF NOT (`there are facts from which it could be decided`)
        THEN `section 204(1) does not help you: there are no facts from which a contravention could be decided absent any other explanation`
        ELSE
            IF `the respondent proves it did not`
            THEN `the respondent proved, on the balance of probabilities, that it did not contravene the Act (s 204(2))`
            ELSE `the respondent is taken to have contravened the Act: there are facts from which that could be decided, absent any other explanation, and the respondent has not proved it did not (s 204(1), (2)); any exemption is for the respondent to prove (s 205)`

-- s 209(1): "If the tribunal decides that the respondent contravened the Act, the tribunal may make 1 or more of the
-- following orders-- (a) ... not to commit a further contravention ...; (b) ... to pay to an affected person, within a
-- specified period, an amount the tribunal considers appropriate as compensation for loss or damage caused by the
-- contravention; (c) ... to do specified things to redress loss or damage ...; (d) ... a private apology or retraction;
-- (e) ... a public apology or retraction ...; (f) ... to implement programs to eliminate unlawful discrimination; (g)
-- ... to pay interest on an amount of compensation; (h) ... declaring void all or part of an agreement made in
-- connection with a contravention". (4): the specified things include employing, reinstating, re-employing or
-- promoting a person, or moving them to a position. (6): "damage ... includes the offence, embarrassment, humiliation,
-- and intimidation suffered by the person." No cap is set. s 154: no reinstatement or re-employment order where the
-- worker applied for industrial relief first. s 210: "After a hearing, the tribunal may make an order dismissing a
-- complaint."
DECLARE `Order` IS ONE OF
    `an order not to commit a further contravention against you (s 209(1)(a))`
    `compensation for your loss or damage, including offence, embarrassment, humiliation and intimidation, paid within a set period, with no cap in the Act (s 209(1)(b), (6))`
    `an order to do specified things to redress your loss or damage, such as employing, reinstating, re-employing or promoting you (s 209(1)(c), (4))`
    `an order to do specified things to redress your loss or damage, but not to reinstate or re-employ you (ss 154, 209(1)(c), (4))`
    `a private apology or retraction (s 209(1)(d))`
    `a public apology or retraction (s 209(1)(e))`
    `an order to implement programs to eliminate unlawful discrimination (s 209(1)(f))`
    `interest on the compensation (s 209(1)(g))`
    `an order declaring void all or part of an agreement made in connection with the contravention (s 209(1)(h))`
    `an order dismissing the complaint (s 210)`

@export What orders can the tribunal make for me, under the Queensland Anti-Discrimination Act 1991 ss 209, 210 and 154? If it decides the respondent contravened the Act: an order not to do it again, compensation (including for offence, embarrassment, humiliation and intimidation, with no cap in the Act), orders to redress your loss such as reinstatement (not if you sought industrial relief first), private or public apologies, programs, interest, and voiding an agreement. Otherwise it may dismiss the complaint.
GIVEN `the tribunal decided the respondent contravened the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the tribunal decided that the respondent contravened the Act
      `I applied for industrial relief first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were dismissed and applied for industrial relief before lodging your complaint (s 154)
GIVETH A LIST OF `Order`
`What orders can the tribunal make for me` `the tribunal decided the respondent contravened the Act` `I applied for industrial relief first` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the tribunal decided the respondent contravened the Act`)
    THEN LIST `an order dismissing the complaint (s 210)`
    ELSE
        LIST `an order not to commit a further contravention against you (s 209(1)(a))`,
             `compensation for your loss or damage, including offence, embarrassment, humiliation and intimidation, paid within a set period, with no cap in the Act (s 209(1)(b), (6))`,
             `the redress order`,
             `a private apology or retraction (s 209(1)(d))`,
             `a public apology or retraction (s 209(1)(e))`,
             `an order to implement programs to eliminate unlawful discrimination (s 209(1)(f))`,
             `interest on the compensation (s 209(1)(g))`,
             `an order declaring void all or part of an agreement made in connection with the contravention (s 209(1)(h))`
    WHERE
        `the redress order` MEANS
            IF `I applied for industrial relief first`
            THEN `an order to do specified things to redress your loss or damage, but not to reinstate or re-employ you (ss 154, 209(1)(c), (4))`
            ELSE `an order to do specified things to redress your loss or damage, such as employing, reinstating, re-employing or promoting you (s 209(1)(c), (4))`
